sections.body:
Because arrays are reference types (see the Data Types concept), assigning one array variable to another does not copy the array. It copies the reference, so both variables point at the same array:

```javascript
const original = [1, 2, 3];
const alias = original;
alias.push(4);
console.log(original);   // [1, 2, 3, 4], changed even though you only touched `alias`
```

This is usually unintentional: someone writes `const copy = original` meaning to make an independent copy, then mutates `copy` (via `push`, `splice`, index assignment, or another mutating method) and is surprised that `original` changed too. There was never a copy, only one array with two names. To make an independent (shallow) copy, use `original.slice()`, `[...original]` (spread, covered fully in a later concept), or `Array.from(original)` -- each creates a new array object with the same elements, so mutating the copy leaves the original untouched.

examples.code:
const scores = [10, 1, 2, 21];
const result = scores.sort();

console.log(result);   // [1, 10, 2, 21], sorted as strings, not numbers
console.log(scores);   // [1, 10, 2, 21], the original array was mutated too
console.log(result === scores);  // true, same array, two names

const fixed = [10, 1, 2, 21].sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(fixed);    // [1, 2, 10, 21], correct numeric order

const cart = ["apple", "bread"];

const alias = cart;
alias.push("milk");
console.log(cart);    // ["apple", "bread", "milk"], unintentionally changed

const realCopy = cart.slice();
realCopy.push("eggs");
console.log(cart);      // ["apple", "bread", "milk"], untouched this time
console.log(realCopy);  // ["apple", "bread", "milk", "eggs"]

