sections.body:
Like arrays, plain objects are reference types: a variable holding an object holds a reference to it, not the object's data directly. Assigning `const b = a` for an object `a` copies that reference, so `b` and `a` become two names for the same object, not two independent objects:

```javascript
const a = { count: 1 };
const b = a;
b.count = 99;
console.log(a.count);   // 99, a and b were always the same object
```

This affects comparison too: `===` on two objects checks whether they're the *same reference*, not whether their contents match. `{ x: 1 } === { x: 1 }` is `false`: two separately created objects are never `===` equal, even with identical contents, because they're different objects in memory. Only `a === b` where both names refer to the same object (as in the snippet above) is `true`.

A key inside an object literal can be computed from an expression by wrapping it in `[ ]` at the point you define the object, instead of setting it afterward via bracket-notation assignment:

```javascript
const field = "score";
const result = { [field]: 100, [`${field}Max`]: 150 };
// equivalent to { score: 100, scoreMax: 150 }
```

This is useful when building an object dynamically, for instance a lookup table keyed by a runtime value (a user ID, a category name), without a separate assignment after the literal is created.

examples.code:
const product = { name: "Widget", "unit-price": 9.99 };

console.log(product.name);           // "Widget", a valid identifier, so dot works
console.log(product["unit-price"]);  // 9.99, a hyphen isn't valid in an identifier, so dot fails here
// console.log(product.unit-price); // this would be parsed as (product.unit) - price, not what you want

const key = "name";
console.log(product[key]);   // "Widget", key is a variable, so bracket notation is required

const settings = {
  theme: "dark",
  notifications: { email: true, sms: false }
};

const userSettings = { ...settings };
userSettings.theme = "light";                  // independent, top-level string
userSettings.notifications.sms = true;         // mutates the SHARED nested object

console.log(settings.theme);                    // "dark", unaffected
console.log(settings.notifications.sms);        // true, unintentionally changed!

// A fix for the nested object:
const safeCopy = { ...settings, notifications: { ...settings.notifications } };

