sections.body:
Before promises existed, asynchronous operations in JavaScript were handled with **callbacks**: functions passed in to be called later, once an operation finished. That works for one async step, but chaining several dependent steps meant nesting callbacks inside callbacks:

```javascript
getUser(userId, function(user) {
  getPosts(user.id, function(posts) {
    getComments(posts[0].id, function(comments) {
      console.log(comments);
      // ...and error handling has to be duplicated at every level
    }, function(err) { console.error(err); });
  }, function(err) { console.error(err); });
}, function(err) { console.error(err); });
```

This pattern, nicknamed **'callback hell,'** has two problems beyond looks. The nesting grows one level for every additional async step, so the code gets harder to read top to bottom; and error handling has to be wired up at every level, since an error at one step has no shared way to propagate to a common handler. Promises, and later async/await, were built to solve both.

`async`/`await` lets you write promise-based code that reads like synchronous code, without changing what happens underneath; it's built on the promise mechanism already described. Marking a function `async` does two things. Inside its body you can use `await somePromise` to pause that function at that line until the promise settles, then continue with the resolved value (no `.then()` callback needed). And **an `async` function always returns a promise**, a common surprise even when its body is a plain `return 5;`. The returned value is wrapped in a fulfilled promise; to get the plain `5` back out, the caller still needs to `await` the call (from inside another async function) or `.then()` it, as with any promise.

```javascript
async function getValue() {
  return 5;
}

console.log(getValue());          // Promise { 5 } (Node's format), not 5 directly
getValue().then(v => console.log(v));   // 5
```

`await` only pauses the `async` function it's inside; it does not block the rest of the program. Other code (other functions, other event handlers) keeps running while one async function is paused at an `await`, which is why `await` never freezes the page the way a long synchronous loop would. `try`/`catch` is the async/await equivalent of `.catch()`: wrapping an `await` in a `try` block catches that promise's rejection as a regular thrown error, so you handle async errors with the same syntax as synchronous ones.

A common performance bug: awaiting inside a loop over several **independent** async operations, which forces them to run one at a time when nothing requires that:

```javascript
// SLOW: each fetch waits for the previous one to finish first
const results = [];
for (const id of userIds) {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);   // pauses the loop until this one resolves
  results.push(user);
}
```

Each iteration's `await` pauses the loop until that fetch resolves before *starting* the next one, so three independent one-second fetches take three seconds in sequence, though none depends on the others and they could all be in flight at once. The fix is to start every operation first (no awaiting inside the loop), collect the promises, and then wait for all of them together with **`Promise.all`**:

```javascript
// FAST: all fetches start immediately, running concurrently
const promises = userIds.map(id => fetchUser(id));
const results = await Promise.all(promises);   // waits for all of them together
```

`userIds.map(...)` calls `fetchUser` for every id immediately, with no waiting in between, so every request is in flight before `Promise.all` is reached; `Promise.all` then waits for all of those promises and resolves to an array of their results, in the same order as the input. One caveat: `Promise.all` **rejects as soon as any single promise rejects**, discarding the results of the ones that had already succeeded. If you need every outcome regardless of individual failures, `Promise.allSettled` is the tool: it always resolves with a status/value or status/reason for every promise and never rejects itself.

